h; might need adjustment based on actual content\n            if 'publish' in line.lower() or 'date' in line.lower():\n                current_post['date'] = line\n            elif line and not line.startswith('http'):  # Avoid URLs\n                # Assume it's a title if it's not a date and not empty\n                current_post['title'] = line\n                if 'date' in current_post:\n                    posts.append(current_post)\n                    current_post = {}\n    # If we didn't capture properly, fallback: take every two lines as title and date\n    if len(posts) < 3:\n        posts = []\n        for i in range(0, len(lines), 2):\n            if i+1 < len(lines):\n                title = lines[i].strip()\n                date = lines[i+1].strip()\n                if title and date:\n                    posts.append({'title': title, 'date': date})\n    # Now, get the third most post (assuming 'most' means by order, e.g., third in list)\n    if len(posts) >= 3:\n        third_post = posts[2]  # Zero-indexed, so index 2 is third\n        result = f\"Title: {third_post['title']}, Publish Date: {third_post['date']}\"\n    else:\n        result = \"Not enough posts found on the homepage.\"\n    return result\n</code>\n\n<exception>\n'SyntaxError': unterminated string literal (detected at line 2191)\n</exception>\n\n---\n\n<reasoning>\nI need to identify the publish date and title of the third most post on the homepage. Currently, I'm on the homepage at 'http://localhost:8000/' with an accessibility tree showing only a main element containing a figure with an image, and no visible posts. This suggests the homepage might not display posts directly, or they might be loaded dynamically or via links. The task likely refers to blog posts or articles, which may be accessible through navigation. The available functions can help extract content from pages. Specifically, extract_main_content(page, page_url) extracts text from the main element of a given page, whic